In article <4m2a31$22i@filet.nrlssc.navy.mil>, wischow@voodoo.nrlssc.navy.mil () writes:
:
:I am attempting to connect a DEC VT320 to a modem.  Not suprisingly the pins on
:the VT320 are weird!!!  Does anyone have the pin connection for VT320 to a DB25
:connector (with modem control) ???

  The pinout of the MMJ -- I assume that's the "weird" pinout you are
  referencing -- is present in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions
  (FAQ) monthly posting.  The FAQ is available at the following:

    comp.os.vms newsgroup
    comp.answers newsgroup
    news.answers newsgroup
    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms
    ftp://rtfm.mit.edu/pub/usenet/news.answers/dec-faq/vms
    CompuServe VAXFORUM, Library 0, VMSFAQ.TXT

  Various MMJ adapters are also available and a number are listed in
  if FAQ -- I'd consider getting a DB25-to-MMJ for the modem, and using
  the BC16-series MMJ-MMJ cabling to connect from the VT320 to the modem.

  The MMJ looks similar to a standard US modular telephone jack, though
  the latch-notch is offset to the side to prevent the incorrect (and
  potentially damaging) insertion of a telephone line into the jack.

  I generally don't bother with the modem control signals between the
  terminal and the modem -- it's the host-end modem wiring that most
  often needs to concern itself with dropped modem connections.

