Subject: PDP-8/e fan repair
Date: 1996/04/30
Message-ID: <4m464s$mrd@access1.digex.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 152169776
organization: Society for the Obsolescence of Obsolescence
newsgroups: alt.sys.pdp8,comp.sys.dec


While attempting to disassemble one of my PDP-8/e's fans, which is
stuck - the bearings appear to be frozen, I discovered something.  It
appears that the fan in the 8/e is quite different, mechanically, from
a Boxer fan in my 11/44 CPU that I repaired (thanks to very helpful
advice from A.R.D.), which uses ball bearings.  Am I correct to guess,
based upon what I've seen thus far, that these Boxer fans in the 8/e
use sleeve bearings instead of ball bearings?  If so, should I use a
different type of lubricant on them?

Here's what I've done so far to remove the fan blade and commutator
assembly:

1. Remove the circlip that holds the small metal disk/dust cover in place.
   If you don't have the right type of pliers to remove this, the ends of
   a bent paper clip inserted into the holes of the circlip, and squeezed
   together using a pair of nose-pincher pliers, works quite well. 
2. Remove the thin metal disk/dust cover (printing facing out).
3. Remove the metal disk with a rubber coating on it (circle facing out)
4. Remove the metal cap - pull out by inserting a bent paper clip (one of
   my favorite, and least expensive, tools) into the hole in the midle and
   pull it out.
5. Remove the amber plastic disk.
6. Remove the felt ring.
7. Remove the small circlip from the shaft.
8. Remove small black metal washer.
9. Pull fan blade and commutator assembly off of the shaft.

At this point, I attempted to remove the bearing assembl(y)(ies) from
the commutator in order to soak it in mineral spirits and then
lubricate it with high-melting point grease, but I couldn't get it to
budge.  It's stuck rather tightly.  A few minutes ago, I sprayed some
WD-40 on it, and will spray some more on it before I retire for the
night.  Hopefully it will have loosened up a bit by later in the
morning.

It's true that I could have gone to some random electronics store,
even a Rat Shack store, and purchased a new fan, or sent away for a
surplus fan.  However, I'd rather use the original fan.  Besides, I'm
one of those who doesn't belive in replacing something that can be
repaired - we need to do away with this "don't fix it, throw it away
and buy a new one" mentality which is making a mess of things.

