>FWD disks in it.
>        A scsi bus requires termination at both ends of the bus, this would
>be provided by the adaptors.  In the description of the scsi cluster there
>is a section called: 
>"Building a System That Allows a Server to Be Removed (Using DWZZA 
Converters)."
>Wherein (as far as my poor brain can understand) the DWZZAs are used to 
>provide termination allowing one or the other server to be removed.
> 

  the dwzza is primarily a signal converted but it also supplies
  bus termination as well. THis is required when your scsi bus
  is segmented (differential / single ended)

       I am not interested in physically removing a server but I would like
>to bring one or the other down for system backup/restore or upgrading VMS 
or 
>maybe even all the way off to do maintenance on it.  The adaptor will still
>be there and the other adaptor will be providing power to the bus.  Is this
>a problem? 

  Doing this is not a problem unless you physically remove the KZPSA
  controller when it's supplying bus termination. Then, that's a problem.

> Or do I really need the DWZZAs in there?

  You need a dwzza if you have an 'incompatibility' between the scsi
  controller and the devices. In your case, you say you have FWD
  devices in a 3rd party disk enclosure. You also mentioned that you
  have KZPSAs SCSI controllers (which are FWD). So, I really don't
  understand the need for a DWZZA. Are you really sure you have
  FWD devices ?? (Most are single-ended)





