In article <4loipt$amf@nntp5.u.washington.edu>,
L. Liu <lliu@u.washington.edu> wrote:
>According to the AlphaStation 250 series' manual, the workstation uses
>72-pin SIMM with parity bit (70ns or 60 ns). I'm wondering what's the
>difference between the 72-pin SIMM for AlphaStation and the 72-pin SIMM
>for PC? (PC has 72-pin SIMM with parity bit, too.) And what's the
>difference between the SIMM used by AlphaStation 200 series and the
>SIMM used by the 250 series? Are they interchangable? I appreciate any
>answers. Thank you for your time.

As far as I can tell, you can use standard tin-lead 72-pin parity SIMM's
in the AlphaStation 200 series.  Most PC's don't seem to come with
parity memory these days, in fact, the Triton chipset doesn't use
the parity bit.  The manual that came with it says that 60ns or 70ns
will work.

About the 250 series, I'm not sure.  I suspect it's the same deal.
I think the 600 series might use a different sort of memory.

Regards,

PeterM




