In article <jones-1804961322110001@eblm01.ebl.rl.ac.uk>, jones@vi.rl.ac.uk (Brian Jones) says:
>
>I have some 5.25" floppies written years ago on a DECMATE word processor. 
>Any body know how to read on a PC floppy drive?  I only want text.
>
>-- 
>Email jones@vi.rl.ac.uk (Brian Jones)
First thing you need, is to be able to read the floppy format.
The Decmate uses 400K single sided floppies, which no current
PC uses. If you can find something that can read Ranbow/MS-DOS
floppies, it should work. There used to be commercial programs
to do this, but I don't know of any source at the moment.

The second problem you'll have, is something to translate the
floppy disk's format into something usable. It needs to understand
the directory structure, and the weird encoding format used
on the floppies (it stores the characters in a funny format,
since it is essentially a 12 bit format)

I can't help you with the first part, but I did write a program
that can handle the second part a long time ago.
It inputs from a floppy drive mounted using one of the
Rainbow Floppy Drivers, and outputs a bunch of WordPerfect
format files, keeping a lot of the formatting, handling overprinted
characters, and many other fun things.

If you want it, please send me email telling me where I can
place this program's source code (don't have a place where I
can place it for you, and it's too long for this news group).

You'll need a C compiler to compile it (it was written using
Borland C), and may need to edit it for changes in the compiler
since the time I wrote it, but I was rather happy with it at the time.




