Ben Cannon <artherd@a.crl.com> writes:

| jum@anubis.han.de (Jens-Uwe Mager) wrote:
| >In article <4l0qbr$lel@onramp.arc.nasa.gov>, lamaster@viking.arc.nasa.gov
| >(Hugh LaMaster) wrote:
| >
| >> Third, you have to be careful interpreting any benchmark.  For example,
| >> I reboot systems fairly often, and the SGI Indy systems I reboot come
| >> up faster than any desktop I have used [with a "real" OS], despite the 
| >> fact that some numbers would lead to expect otherwise.  I don't know
| >> why they reboot more quickly, and it doesn't seem to matter to many people, 
| >> but it happens to be an important performance criterion *to me*.  YMMV.
| 
| I remenber vividly the day I turned on an Indy.  By the time the monitor's guns 
| were warmed up, the machine had booted it's whole self and my own image was 
| staring at me form the little indycam window. Awesome. (I had a 386 PC at the 
| time :)

A lot of people at SGI spent a lot of effort making those reboots
fast (and it's something you have to keep working on, as people
add more features ;))

We reboot them a lot too, during development, and we also wanted people
to have the feeling that they could turn them off when not using them,
if they wanted to, without much penalty when they needed to use them.
(People tend to do that more in homes and small offices, and also more
in Europe, than in the US) or so I've been told.

Our large systems, on the other hand, are rather painfully slow to
boot...  There have been many times I've been ready to reset them,
convinced they were hung, by the time they make it up.  Too much
effort on the diags, with no way to bypass them, in my opinion (good
to have when you need them, but not on ever boot and reboot!).

[ getting marginal as regards processors, but it does point out that
systems feature sets and performance are not just the speed with which
they run apps ;) ]
