SYSTEM MANAGER wrote:
> 
> Hello all,
> 
>         I am considering setting up a scsi cluster using two alphaservers
> with kzpsa pci-scsi FWD adaptors going to a third party disk enclosure with
> FWD disks in it.
>         A scsi bus requires termination at both ends of the bus, this would
> be provided by the adaptors.  In the description of the scsi cluster there
> is a section called:
> "Building a System That Allows a Server to Be Removed (Using DWZZA Converters)."
> Wherein (as far as my poor brain can understand) the DWZZAs are used to
> provide termination allowing one or the other server to be removed.I think (from memory) that the DWZZA's are only necessary if you are using
KZPAA's for your SCSI interconnect. Now that KZPSA's are supported, you probably
don't want to use a KZPAA. 
>         I am not interested in physically removing a server but I would like
> to bring one or the other down for system backup/restore or upgrading VMS or
> maybe even all the way off to do maintenance on it.  The adaptor will still
> be there and the other adaptor will be providing power to the bus.  Is this
> a problem?  Or do I really need the DWZZAs in there?You don't need any extra hardware to allow powering down one of your nodes.
Moreover, the standard way of connecting and terminating the FWD SCSI at the 
KZPSA's uses the H885-AA TriLink connector and H879-AA Terminator. These allow
disconnecting the bus form the KZPSA without disrupting the termination of the bus.
Hence you can change a bad KZPSA while maintaing connectivity to the KZPSA at the
other end of the bus.
>         I assume I don't want to try to connect to the disks on the scsi
> cluster bus while I am in backup mode?I'm not quite sure what you mean here, but you can still access the disks on the
bus from one node with the other node powered down, or disconnected.
You may run into trouble when you power up the node however, if it does a SCSI
reset. I'm not familiar enough with the behaviour of SCSI clusters to know how
well OpenVMS handles this.
-- 
