From: lou@sun1.giac.montana.edu (Louis Glassy)
Subject: AXP 250 4/266: Death by TULIP Poisoning
Date: 1996/04/22
Message-ID: <LOU.96Apr22083934@sun1.giac.montana.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 150801600
distribution: local
organization: montana.edu
followup-to: comp.sys.dec
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Hello:

Have any of you seen the following situation with a 
AXP Model 250 4/266:

I changed the ethernet port from the BNC port to 
the Twisted-Pair port by entering

	set ewa0_mode twisted

at the SRC monitor prompt, then plugged the system into
a 4-port dumb UTP hub.  All was well.  The system booted
and ran as expected, and was able to use the new network
adapter port without problems.  Then I wanted to 
change things back to the way they were, so halted the
system, unplugged the UTP connection, plugged in the 
BNC connection again, and entered

	set ewa0_mode aui

and booted, and lo, my '250 has died of TULIP poisoning,
and can no longer see the network!

On the console, I get the following message in the early
part of the boot sequence -- I never saw this before:

	tu0: console mode: selecting AUI (10Base5) port: no carrier

The message I used to get (and still do get on my other '250s) was

	tu0: console mode: selecting AUI (10Base5) port

What is going on?  Does switching to UTP permanently disable the 
BNC port?

The OS I'm running is OSF/1 v3.2c.  'uname -n' returns

	OSF1 mango.giac.montana.edu V3.2 148 alpha

and the firmware revision at the SRC prompt is 

	version    X5.0-29 Jul 18 1995 16:20:48

I've checked the BNC network wire.  It's ok, and other machines
can use it without problems.  I've replaced the BNC tee connection,
and other machines can still use this segment without problems.
So I think the thinwire segment is ok.

I moved the AXP to a different (known working) thinwire segment,
and it still gives the 'no carrier' message...

From the unix level, I've removed and re-added tu0 as the network
adapter, using /usr/sbin/setup.  No dice.  The problem appears to me
that something has fried the BNC connection or circuitry in TULIP
(DE435) adapter.  (Visually, the BNC port looks ok -- the probe in the
center is neither bent nor pushed in nor pulled out.)

What else would it be smart for me to check for?  The system's still
under warranty, but in about 6 months I expect to permanently migrate
all 4 of our AXPs to UTP, and I'm uncertain about the sanity of the
250's hardware.  (In about 3 months of use, we lost one keyboard and
had a bad simm in a 2nd AXP system.)


--
Louis Glassy (lou@giac1.oscs.montana.edu), MRC/GIAC Systems Support  
Mountain Research Center, Geographic Information and Analysis Center
222 Traphagen Hall, Montana State University, Bozeman MT 59715 USA



