In article <4lpvmb$jfk@serra.unipi.it>, alex@gateway.unipv.it (Alessandro Bruciamonti) writes:
>Hello all! 
>Apologies for the simple question: I'm a experienced (10 years) Unix 
>administrator, but almost a newbie on DECs.
>
>I'm administering a network of 6 nodes: 3 Unix and 3 DEC workstation
>under VMS (and OPEN-VMS).
>
>I'm running into a problem: when I try to redirect the output of an X11 
>client from Unix workstation to DECwindows workstations I get
>the message:  "Xterm: client is not authorized to use display".
>(or something like)...
>
>When I try to redirect the output of a DECwindows client to an X11 (unix)
>server, all works well and I can see proper output (via TCP/IP, of course).
>
>I've set up proper communication (UCX-TCP-DECNET) protocols, services
>and communication proxyes on VMS: all appears to work ok ...
>
>In addition, I "compile" the form Options/Security of the Session Manager
>of the "receiving" DEC workstations, in order to set up security options 
>for all users/hosts that are entitled to use the DEC displays, but there
>is no way to see X11 output from Unix workstation to DECs.

You need to set the security access on the VMS node. From the session 
manager window select the security entry from the options menu. Enter the 
nodename of the remote unix machine in the node box a * in the Username box
and TCPIP in the transport box. Click on Apply.
 

