In article <4lou93$5mp@scotsman.ed.ac.uk> ajmy@tattoo.ed.ac.uk (A Myles) writes:
>I've just acquired a (rather well equipped it appears) VS3200 which
>I plan to use for fun and to expand my meagre VMS knowledge.
>
>Anyway, despite having about 8ft of orange folders and the TK50 users
>guide, I can't seem to find anything about how much force the cartridge
>release lever requires to lift it to the open position.
>
>A lot it seems - in fact so much that I've not dared to fully lift it.
>
>How much is alot? Hmmm... I think I'd be applying enough force to lift
>a good few kilos. I can get it half way, and can see the mechanisms moving
>clear, but I get worried at about that point.
>

A few kilos!!! Ouch!

The lever is locked in the down position by a release solenoid. The
TK50 must be powered up first, and the unit must pass its self-tests
before it will release the lever.

For what its worth, if there is no cartride in the drive, you should
be able to release the lever by depressing the white catch that is on
the inside lower right of the cartridge slot.

If there is a cartridge in the drive, and the lever won't release when
the power is on and the red button is in the 'out' position, the drive
may be defective, or the something bad happened to the tape.

Hope this helps...

Philippe Pouliquen
(philippe@hermes.ece.jhu.edu)




