-------------------- VCB02 Pinouts --------------------

Holding the M7169 module so that you are looking "into" the
connectors and the components are up, you should see the
following "Berg" connector ("xx" marks the "key" location):

         19 17 15 13 11  9  7  5  3  1
_________20 18 16 xx 12 10  8  6  4  2_________


Berg                                         DB-15 
Pin    Signal Name                           Pin

 1     +12 v for tablet and/or mouse          8
 2     Serial data FROM keyboard             14
 3     Keyboard ground                        6
 4     Serial data TO keyboard               15
 5     -12v for mouse (unused for tablet)    12
 6     +5v for mouse (unused for tablet)      4
 7     Mouse/Tablet ground                    7
 8     Serial data TO mouse/tablet            5
 9     Serial data FROM mouse/tablet         13
10     Unused                                
12     Unused                                
11     Unused                                
13     Unused                                
14     locating key                          
15     Blue video ground                      1
16     Blue video                            11
17     Green video ground                     2
18     Green video                           10
19     Red video ground                       3
20     Red video                              9

All the above assumes that you are using a color monitor
with a BC18Z-xx cable.   If you want to use a monochrome
monitor and cable (BC18P-xx) there are some differences
(see below).   I simply made a DB-15 to DB-15 adapter.
You can also use the color BC18Z-xx cable and take the
monochrome signal off the Green BNC connector (like I am
using right now).

As for the monochrome cabinet kit, this is a bit more
complicated.   If I fail to make it clear, let me know.

DEC sold two different cabinet kits with the GPX boards, one
for color systems and one for mono.   The monitor cables
were also wired differently (besides having a different
number of video connectors).   The boards, however, were
identical.

If you had a color cabinet kit and cable, you could simply
attach the GREEN video connector to the mono monitor.

The monochrome cabinet kit had the video signal wired
differently, however.   The video signal came off pins 
17 & 18 (GREEN signal) of the Berg connector, but then
were wired to pins 3 & 9 (RED) of the DB-15 connector!
The monochrome cable (BC18P-xx) had the single video
BNC connector wired to these pins (3 & 9), so nobody
really noticed.

The problem came in when someone wanted to use a monochrome
monitor AND CABLE with a color GPX cabinet kit.   Since the
mono cable was expecting video to be on DB-15 pins 3 & 9, it
actually picked up the RED color signal.   Unfortunately,
the RED signal has no sync information on it!

Therefore, if you have a color GPX cabinet kit and a mono
cable, you will have to make an adapter.   I used a pair
of DB-15 connectors separated by short stand-offs.   All
pins were wired straight through except that pin 2 of the
female was wired to pin 3 of the male, and pin 10 of the
female was wired to pin 9 of the male.   The female
connector goes to the GPX cabinet kit, while the male
goes to the monitor cable.

> A question that you might be able to answer ... I was
> experimenting with the graphics cards (thinking I might be
> able to find the pins using an oscilloscope), and the
> MicroVAX wouldn't make it through its diagnostics.  Would
> this be normal for a system that doesn't have a
> keyboard/mouse/monitor hooked up?  It stopped on the
> diagnostic code that meant a problem with the graphics
> system (I forget the code). 

That is the normal, expected behavior.   What is happening
is that the GPX board does its self-test when it is powered
up.   Part of that involves initializing the keyboard and
mouse.   If the proper response signals are not received
from both the keyboard and mouse, the GPX board fails the
self-test and so informs the CPU.   I believe that the
displayed code is "A".

