In article <stephenkDq4sI6.Bw9@netcom.com>,
Stephen Knilans <stephenk@netcom.com> wrote:

>Actually, memory tests HAVE to be SLOW!  The memory tests most systems do
>...
>Polling for unknown hardware, etc,,, ALSO takes a long time.  FURTHER, my
 
I would expect these functions to be performed by diagnostic or configuration
programs that are invoked explicitly when required, not automatically for every
boot. (This may depend on how critical your application is.  Pilots perform
pre-flight checkout every time; car drivers just hop in and drive away. Maybe
this is a bad example if you look at accident statistics... but you get my
point.)

>WHAT kernel are you working on that takes 10 seconds to boot or less?  I mean
>UNIX generally takes longer than that, ESPECIALLY after a crash.
 
It is an old experimental operating system, from a project killed 10 years ago,
that a few people have kept alive for their own use.  But you bring up another
point that's especially critical when doing system software development: the
file system must be unconditionally *safe*, so you don't have to worry about
the persistent state on disk being left in an inconsistent state (and hence
have no need for fsck).  Our system has this -- and hence doesn't need the
concept of shutdown either.  Pulling the plug works just fine (assuming the
hardware tolerates it, i.e. parks the disk heads before it's too late).
 
The other person who uses this system actually showed it off at SHARE in Feb95,
running on a P/370 card in an old PS/2 frame.  He demonstrated that one can 
still do exciting things with a S/370...
 
Michel.




