That should indeed cut out the few-hundred milliseconds it may take to boot
from a rotating disk, as well as the spin-up time after power-on.  Even when
the kernel is bloated, this phase of boot tends to take only a few seconds.
What matters to boot time is the gratuitous hardware checkout time before
the physical boot, and system initialisation time after the kernel has been
loaded into memory.
 
But it would be neat to have my system on a Notebook with a flash boot rom.
This might cut boot time to under a second.  (Here I'm assuming that the flash
rom is big enough also to include the root file system, e.g. a few Meg.)
 



