Consider system software development, in particular kernel work.  *Somebody*
has to do this.  This is where fast-boot would really be appreciated.  I may
reboot *deliberately* several times per hour.  I may have just inserted a 
bad bug into the kernel and crash during a test boot -- and I want to be back
up with my working kernel *now*, not later.  I know, I could use two machines,
but why should I have to?
 
Consider a Personal Computer.  I turn it on, work on it for a few minutes,
and turn it off.  (I just had an idea and wanted to try it out.)

Believe me, I would never promote fast-boot as a pain-killer for users of
crappy software...  it would not be appreciated, their pain thresholds must
already be quite high.
 
Michel.





