stephenk@netcom.com (Stephen Knilans) writes:

>Unless the clock is wrong, or the cache(or support circuitry) is too slow, a 150
>Mhz pentium(on a triton board) SHOULD be CLOSE to 50% faster than a 100Mhz, when
>running a program smaller than the cache memory.  Otherwise, of course, the
>memory is too much of a bottleneck, and the faster processors will appraoch a
>near negligable improvement.

>How about it?  Is triton and the pipeline cache fast enough to support the 150?
>How about the 200?  If NOT, then why doesn't intel increase the L1 cache to 
>compensate?  BTW FPU speed should CERTAINLY be about 50% faster REGARDLESS!  
>Is this the case?

>How about it?

What you seem to be assuming is that the 100 MHz P5 and the 150 MHz P5
share the same memory bus speed.  The 100 MHz P5 runs at 1.5 * 66 MHz
while the 150 MHz P5 runs at 3 * 50 MHz, not the expected 2.5 * 60 MHz.
This leads the 150 to be much more memory bound than the 'slower' 100.

Cheers,
David



