>>>SGI_Pow_Chal_90           1   142.1     141.9     144.3     142.2
>>> 
>>>DEC_8400_5/300            8   899.8     834.2     892.8     978.8
>>>DEC_8400_5/300            1   186.3     174.4     198.3     195.2

>>So much for this being a main memory bandwidth benchmark!

>Is your problem understanding why more CPUs give better performance ?
>These are multiprocessors with pended buses, so more CPUs mean more
>simultaneous outstanding bus transactions and hence more memory traffic.
>A part of the reason why the IBM 590/990 numbers are so good is that
>is a uni-processor with very long cache lines. No SMP system can reasonably
>afford the long cache lines they use, especially for commercial workloads.
>It should be noted that IBM themselves has been unable for years to
>even remotely repeat the memory system of these two machines in anything
>newer.

One thing that biases these two particular IBM machines to this test is
that their long cache lines (128 bytes?) lead to very good stride-1
performance.  If this benchmark had accessed memory in a pseudo-random
order, I doubt that they would have been in the upper 50% as the long
cache lines would have killed them.  The Crays, on the other hand, would
maintain a large amount of their huge BW as they use multiple banks 
(interleafing) to keep memory contention low and BW high.

So, don't mistake the above results to say that these two IBM machines
had better "memory performance" than all current workstations.  They
had incredible stride-1 performance.  The test says no more.

Cheers,
David



