In article <17APR199614553046@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu>, ecf_stbo@jhuvms.hcf.jhu.edu (Old-Fashioned Staffordshire Plate...) writes:
|> In article <4l2e5n$umu@usenet.pa.dec.com>, neideck@nestvx.enet.dec.com (Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz) writes...
|> >>So much for this being a main memory bandwidth benchmark!
|> >>
|> >Is your problem understanding why more CPUs give better performance ?
|> >These are multiprocessors with pended buses, so more CPUs mean more
|> >simultaneous outstanding bus transactions and hence more memory traffic.
|>
|> I can see how it might be related to, as you say, 'usable' memory bandwidth but
|> have some questions. Why does it go up almost linearly with number of CPUs?
|> Couldn't one cpu still queue multiple outstanding transactions?  On the 8 cpu
|> systems: are they running 8 copies of the benchmark or a single
|> autoparallelized one?

You've stumbled upon one of the problems with current cached CPU
designs.

Even with multiple GB/s of CPU pin bandwidth, and a GB/s or so of
available memory subsystem bandwidth, they can only deliver a couple
hundred MB/s of memory bandwidth to the user application.

This is true even for systems built around the 21164 and UltraSPARC
with the new whizzo memory queue gizmos.  (I'm not gonna count the
UltraSPARC block move instruction until a compiler generates it for a
SPEC code :-) These memory queues seem to only really work to hide L2
cache latency.

The one exception is the RIOS-2 design in the IBM RS/6000 series.
That had to use triple-ported cache chips, long cache lines, and issue
two two-word-at-a-time load/store instructions/cycle.  Even then it
only uses a third of the available pin and memory bus bandwidth...

Haven't seen STREAM numbers for an R10K with a proper memory subsystem
or a PA-8000 yet, maybe the out-of-order goop will help some.

