(Patrick Gainer) wrote:

> Just for fun, I timed a Challenge S I am using as a client 
> for some experiments and I was able to perform an "init 6"
> (shutdown -r on AIX) and have the machine up again in 42 seconds.
> 
> This is much faster than I can reboot my wife's PowerPC Mac 
> laptop.

Well, it depends on what she has installed. If she has a lot of emulated
extensions it could take longer. Remember, too, that much of what's
happening when the Mac boots is not included in your Challenge boot time.
Many Mac INITS make changes in the system software as they boot--in effect
running a mini-application every time.

BTW, I have a PowerMac 7500 with a ton of extensions and it's not much
more than a minute. I'll trade that extra 20 seconds for the ease of use,
any day. Besides, who really cares?  I walk in the door, hit the power
button on the computer, pick up my coffee cup and head for the break room.
By the time I get my tea and return, the computer's ready to go. Why
should I care if it was a few seconds faster?

-- 
