>BTW, I have a PowerMac 7500 with a ton of extensions and it's not much
>more than a minute. I'll trade that extra 20 seconds for the ease of use,
>any day. Besides, who really cares?  I walk in the door, hit the power
>button on the computer, pick up my coffee cup and head for the break room.
>By the time I get my tea and return, the computer's ready to go. Why
>should I care if it was a few seconds faster?
 
I'm always amazed at how people tolerate slow boot.  Some people get all
excited about 15-second boot times.  That's nice, and better than the two
or three minutes that some other systems take...
 
I happen to consider any boot time of more than two or three seconds too
long.  I understand that hardware (e.g. display) has to warm up, but even
that should not take more than a couple of seconds...
 
Where I really appreciate fast boot is when doing system software development.
I'm likely to break things and crash, so I like to be back up as fast as
possible.  I also like to be able to fix a kernel bug, recompile, rebuild
the kernel, and reboot with a new kernel in the time it takes to enter two
or three commands, i.e. under 10 seconds.
 
Michel.






