In article <00001be4+00001892@msn.com>, JohnnyO@msn.com (John Olavesen) writes:
>Is it possible to dynamically allocate memory in using VAX-11 Macro 
>assembly language?  If so, how would you go about doing it?

Sure it is.  The assembler on OpenVMS Vax and compiler on OpenVMS Alpha do it
all the time.

Here's a simple user-mode, IPL 0, OpenVMS case:

	pushab	my$memory_block		; Address of the allocated block to here
	pushl	#512			; How much I want (in bytes)
	calls	#2,lib$get_vm		; Ask the system for it
	blbs	r0,okay			; Go off and use it
not_ok:					; Deal with maybe not getting it...

And when you're done with it...

	pushab	my$memory_block		; Address of address of block
	pushl	#512			; Size of block (in bytes)
	calls	#2,lib$free_vm		; Fly, be free!

It occurs to me that you may not be working on an OpenVMS system, but any
other operating system that supports an VAX-11 MACRO assembler will also
have in it someplace primitives for memory allocation and deallocation.
Call them in the approved fashion and away you go.

--
Me, not Digital

