In article <DpnL1A.L2H.0.staffin.dcs.ed.ac.uk@dcs.ed.ac.uk>, rcm@dcs.ed.ac.uk (Richard Morrell) writes:
>  I recently managed to get a 240Mb Maxtor MFM hard disk, but my uVax 
> doesn't see to like it.  Could anyone help me with the following:

  Posting a model number would be helpful 8-). There's no such thing as a
240MB Maxtor MFM model. There's an XT-2190 which comes out to about 233MB
(as I recall) if you format it 2,7 RLL (it's about 160MB in MFM mode).

> 1) My diagnostic software claims that the controller card (RQDX3) is not 
> working, but gives no explaination as to why.  However, the diagnostic 
> software can still detect the presence of a hard disk.  It first reports 
> it as a RD51 in the system description, then as a RD54 when I have a 
> choice to format it.

  Assuming you have an XT-2190, this is normal behavior - the RQDX3 writes
the disk type on the drive, and all drives without the secret format code
are identified as RD51's. The earlier RQDX1/2 controllers thought they could
determine the drive type by sending various illegal commands to the drive,
but the RD53 (Micropolis 1325) had to have a special jumper designed in for
this purpose (as it was 1024 x 8, there were no illegal addresses).

>  2) The format works OK until about 11 minutes into the format.  The 
> drive beeps and the diagnostics say that there was an error in formating 
> and then bails out.

  Again, giving the specific error would be helpful. I suspect it's an error
writing the RCT.

  There are 3 possibilities here:

  1) you're using the customer formatter, not the DEC Field Service one. It
     can only reformat drives with existing good RQDX3 formats (duh!).
  2) You have an RQDX3 controller with firmware older than F4, or with the
     RWC/HEDSEL jumpers set improperly.
  3) If asked, you responded "no" to "continue if RCT is inaccessible" or
     any of the other related questions.

  See ftp://ftp.spc.edu/third-party-disks.txt for more details.

