: Right.

 We where talking about the x86 and risc like a PA or PPC....
 CISC (and RISC) are not all alike.

: >RISCs instructions are (typically) simpler and do less

: Right. Note however, that most RISCs features 2 x src->destination type
: operations, whereas most CISCs uses 2-address type operations, with one
: of the addresses acting as both source and destinations. This may make
: extra move operations needed on a CISC.

 Also an example is the shift+add+move of the PA.
 x86 for a cisc dont have very complex instructions.

 I think to copy indexed memory you would do a load & store on a x86(RISC 
 like) VS a move EA,EA on a 680x0.(Some 680x0 inst can take 22byte, and 
 really ALOT. I almost consider the 680x0 as a C interpreter:)

: >The lack of registers is far less important for CISC (why? I have no clue.
: >anybody care to expound on this?)

: Actualle, I don't think you are right. The lack of registers is a
: problem. It can be reduced by on-the-fly register renaming and out of
: order execution - but it is not cheap.

 On a 68040 for example doing a OPERATION   EA,REGISTER is not slower
 then OPERATION	REGISTER,REGISTER . 1 cycle (EA can be some of the 14
 addressing mode the 680x0 support)
 Because of the horrible x86 design some people resort to self modifying
 code to acheive the same result.(This worked ok on x86 unified cache,
 but this kind of behaviour is not necesary on the 680x0 and knowing
 that the 680x0 has a separate inst cache flushing it is defently
 a hit)

 Stephan




