David K. Every <dke@adnc.com> wrote:
[snipt]
: Now you can add L1 or L2 to a CISC processor... but then for the same cost
: you could add MORE to a RISC processor. So in the real world with the
: added cache's of RISC, I beleive the usage of memory is much smaller than
: CISC (say 40%). This is mostly speculation (on the exact numbers) but
: holds true in theory... if it did not, then RISCS would be slower than
: CISC, and this does not seem to be the case.


Mmmm... L2 cost and L1 cost are likely to be the same for RISC and CISC 
(I'm almost 100% positive that L2 costs will be the same since you'd end 
up using the same components at a given data-bus speed).

RISC almost certainly benefits more from them however.

As an example, I did a timing test of L1, L2 and RAM access on a Quadra 
and on a 9500/120.


FOr the Quadra, the difference between L1, L2 and RAM access wasn't 
nearly as great as the difference between L1, L2 and RAM access times on 
the 9500. In fact, in the case where L2 cache-thrash was induced, the 
resulting slow-down made memory access on the 120/40Mhz 9500 nearly as 
slow as it was on the 25Mhz Quadra 660AV.

And I believe that the worst possible case had the 9500 going even slower 
than the 660AV when doing simple memory moves.

It was something like 50x slower than the L1 access times.

If you like, I'll dust off my notes and give you exact figures.






