In article <4kpmq6$8pd@nadine.teleport.com> Stephan Schaem,
sschaem@teleport.com writes:
>: RISC architectures have a fixed instruction length.  The uniform size of
>: an
>: instruction to be fetched makes pipelining easy.
>
> And with compiler cooperation you could set that register usage is
> alway interleaved to issue 2 inst/cycle without any real logic to
> decode in advance if what you will do will afect the next instruction.
> I dont know if any chip actually uses something like this tho? 
>

I'm not sure what you mean here.  Mostly, the compiler needs to resolve
which operations depend on the results of a previous operation. Then, the
compiler will (hopefully) just schedule the instructions far enough apart
so that the pipeline will not stall.  Obviously, two instructions with a
dependency between them can't be issued simultaneously.

A processor implementation can't depend on the compiler to perfectly
schedule
code, so it must always resolve dependencies. 

