>In article <4ko7ml$pl4@kruuna.helsinki.fi> torvalds@cc.helsinki.fi (Linus Torvalds) writes:
>>clocking the thing faster.  They'll have to improve memory performance
>>noticeably (and I'm told they will do that, but I just wanted to point
>
>How can they improve memory performance, and why would they?  They ALREADY
>have MULTIMEGABYTE static caches!  I don't know why they bothered.  Why didn't
>they just go 100% static?  I mean the VAX that we last had had only 9MB, and
>I have seen a LOT of alpha MBs with 4MB cache!
>

I wouldn't want to use an Alpha running Digital Unix with anything
less than 64M of RAM.

64M of static RAM cache would be prohibitively expensive--at least
to a market big enough to keep DEC in business, and many people 
run things requiring much more memory than that.

Memory bandwidth IS important for DEC machines.  Many real-world
programs have either poor cache locality or extremely large data
sets, much larger than 4M.

Spec95 has made an effort to model such applications, and the
21164 has done somewhat less well on Spec95fp than I had expected.

I think that this is due to limitations on the memory bandwidth.
Others of that opinion also, but does anyone know for sure?

Regards,

PeterM



