>yansong
>yxc02740@bayou.uh.edu

I DO know they make 5ns static, and have heard that they supposedly make faster.
That is good enough to support a 100% efficient processor running at 200Mhz!
If they had 4 way interleaving, then sequential access COULD be reduced to
where it would handle a 100% efficient CPU at almost 800Mhz!  That would be 
ASTOUNDING!  All they have to do is have the lower 2 address bits multiplexed
with the chip select pins. 
Considering that the average dynamic ram can't even run at 16Mhz(although 60ns
is CLOSE), that is VERY good.  They could have interleving here to reduce 50ns
memory to effectively become 20ns memory.
OBVIOUSLY, any dynamic ram at that speed will 
cost more, or they would be using it for cache.  Further, dynamic ram speed
can NEVER reach its full potential, because it must undergo a refresh cycle.
So static is the way to go for speed.  BTW I would HOPE that the CPU to cache
latency on an alpha is below 10ns, and the cache to main ram latency is about 
50ns or perhaps better.  I don't know about Triton, et-al, but even NEAT had the
interleave concept.  I would hope DEC has used it.  Heck, some even have 8
way interleaving.  Granted, this can actually SLOW some random addressing, but
instruction prefetch and the L1 cache should reduce the effect of that.  

BTW I DID read about the 64MB requirement et-al, but have you seen the price for
some of these boards?  A 64MB board with all static would cost LESS than $64000!
Granted, that is a lot of money, but if you could make it handle the processor
at near max, it would be worth it to many companies.  My boss told me his
RA81s cost about $20,000, and he had TWO of them!  He also had 2 RA-80s, which
would obviously cost OVER $10,000!  That is $60,000 right THERE!  The cost for
equivalent hardware TODAY?  About $260!  SO, he could take the $59,740 savings,
and buy that alpha!  BTW for a 100Mhz pentium with 64MB, some are selling them
for $5560.  Had DEC, with their old pricing policy, sold a similar system, it
would cost about $43000!  SO, historically, that system would only cost about 
50% more than a pentium, or about 7 times as much as a 1.2GB disk drive( for 
those that don't know, the RA81 is a 400MB dec disk drive and the RA80 is the
same thing only 200MB)(these drives were about 2 feet wide, weighed 350 pounds,
and were as I recall, capable of 2MB/s with 30ms seek)! Today, they make 
3.5" wide drives that have <10ms seek and can easily handle over 7MB/s for $250!

Don't forget, the ALPHA IS sold as a high end product! 

Steve

BTW the 50% more than a pentium, and the 7 times the price of a 1.2GB drive DO
take into account the decrease of prices over the past few years.  If not for
the fact that DEC smartened up a few years ago, and started selling things at
market, these estimates would have been VERY close!  To give you an idea,
those $60,000 disk drives were selling at about 33 times the market price, and
memory was over 7 times the market price.  The PCs were selling at about 2.5 
times the market.  TODAY, surprisingly, their prices are almost 1:1!





