In article <4kr7kp$lif@nadine.teleport.com> Stephan Schaem,
sschaem@teleport.com writes:
> of how risc can do something cisc cannot, and how 'simple' it would be:
> lets consider a instruction set fixed at 32bit per instruction:
> 2 instruction for 64bit... you could design the cpu to execute 2
> instruction in 1 cycle with little effort by having a rule where no
> destination can overlap. So its the compiler job, not the CPU to make 
> sure this is true. the CPU cannot stall on register conflict because all
> operation are fully concurent.
>

Ah, I see.  You have just described a Very Long Instruction Word
architecture,
otherwise known as VLIW.  It is rumored that HP and Intel are looking
into using
this for P7 or P8.  Basically, this type of architecture relies
completely on the
compiler.  VLIW takes your idea to the extreme.  Instructions can be 128
or 256
bits.(whatever you like)  This macro-instruction would have many parallel
operations
embedded within it.  For instance, a single instruction could have 2
integer and two
floating point instructions.  

> Or you can see it as a dual CPU sharing a registerfile and cache but for 
> CPU1 PC = PC+0 , CPU2 PC = PC+4 .

This could be implemented as a general superscalar machine packaged on
separate die
in a multi-chip module.


> The above would be totaly worthless if one find that a huge amount
> of nop need to be inserted. 
>
> BTW, the block can still be considered instructions... So what ever
> you use to aply to instruction you now aply to a 'block'.

Yep.  This is probably the biggest argument against VLIW.  The other is
that
compatibility between successive generations might be difficult if someone
wanted to add capabilities in newer generations.  I don't think it is a
good
long-term idea in its current form.  With some further development and
twists on
this idea, I think it has good possibilities.  I sometimes wonder what
the next big
advance in architecture will be.  It seems as if they've done everything.

Most people don't know it, but many of the techniques used on today's
most advanced
processors were pioneered long ago by mainframe companies in the 60's and
70's.  They
have only become feasible recently on microprocessors because of the
amount of silicon
area they occupy.

