It is marginally less important since each instruction (typically) takes
more clock cycles (so time lost to memory accesses is slightly less of an
issue) -- but the best CISC designs all have a lot of registers
(relatively speaking). The IBM 360 beat most earlier mainframes on speed
because it had more registers.
