Matthias Heiden wrote:
> 
> Hi there,
> 
> Many distributors offer DLT drives at lower prices than DEC.
> The technical specifications are identical.
> 
> Are these drives 100% compatible with DEC's own drives
> on DEC systems, in particular with DEC UNIX ?
> 
> Which sort of problems could I expect if the SCSI inquiry
> command reports a different drive name  ?
> 
> Anyone out there with experience in this field ?

I don't have direct experience, but this is what I've heard.

Mechanicaly, the OEM, Quantum, and DEC DLT drives of similar capabilities 
are identical. For example, I think the DLT2000 and the TZ87 are 
identical drives. The difference is the firmware.

From what I understand, they are pretty much compatible, except that the 
DLT2000 firmware does not read TK50 and TK70 tapes, while the TZ87 
firmware does. (It might be TK70 and TZ85.)

Interestingly, you can turn a DLT2000 into a TZ87 (right down to the 
inquiry string) by loading new firmware into it.  However, you need some 
special utilities from Quantum to do this.

I don't know about compatibility with Digital UNIX. I have heard that VMS 
does not have any problems with the OEM drives. However, I think that 
there are problems with HSC controllers recognizing the drive, 
irrespective of OS.

Remember, these ramblings are just what I have heard, but they may help 
you figure out the questions to ask.

Good luck!

