SGI Indy R4400SC/200 was Best Price Performer in AIM's General Workstation MIX.

See http://www.aim.com/hotiron_spring96.html

MIPS/SGI claim the same; that they optimise their machines for app performance.
I don't think that its the firms that are making a big deal out of it, its us
users who are doing that. In DECs case, users are more guilty of this than many.
They were hyping the Alpha chip lots, and when the initial systems didn't
come up with the goods (IMHO) they were dissappointed. Eventually, DEC produced
systems which had good support (caches/fast bus) for the CPU and the users where
happy again.

This is why benchmarks are (usually) false. They are still useful for justifying
purchases, though. Typically, benchmarks have been too easily taken advantage of.
Manufacturers have optimised machines to run the benchmarks fast because that is
the measure people use to buy them. SGI/MIPS (I think) prefer benchmarks such as
AIM for this very reason. Unfortunately, (or fortunately - depending which group
you are reading) they don't do brilliantly (quite well - but def. not brill.) in
them either. Alpha still seems to have the upper hand (occasionally dropping back).
We'll see how these new SPEC95's measure up.

