"S. Debruynkops" <debk> writes:

>I have the option to buy a used 3000/800 for something less than the price of a
>new 400 4/233 with comparable features.  The machines benchmark about the same
>for my intensive floating point computations.  Typical code consumes 200+ Mb
>memory and runs for several days with perhaps one interactive user also on the
>system.  Operating system is UNIX.  I'd appreciate comments on the relative
>merits of the different bus architectures or other points.   --Steve

`where are you going to be with the system in 2 years?'. I've got a 400 4/233
on my desk, and we've used the 3000_800 as our main fileserver for a while. I
know which pedestal fits on a desk and which doesn't :-). You can fit 4 3.5"
disks in the 3000_800 as well as 2 5.25 devices. (1/2 height, typically
cdrom+tape). You can fit 1G of memory into the 3000_800, but less than that in
the 4/233. (i vaguely remember 6 simm sockets in mine, so a max of either
192M or 384M assuming 32M or 64M simms?)

Throw PCI against Turbochannel. I much prefer the latter, but the newest cards 
are moving towards the former.  the 3000_800 has 6 turbochannel slots, and the
400 4/233 has 3 PCI slots (2 assuming you have network in one... or only one
free one if you have a video card also.. or 0 in my case with a AV301 as well
:-().

Do you care about downtime ? i think the 400 4/233 comes with dec's 3 year warranty
(1 year onsite, 2 rtd). Maintenance on the 3000_800 is very expensive when
you do cost comparisons over 2-3 years.

I'd probably go for the 400 4/233, especially if you might have other uses for
after your particular project.

-jason
