[Zero-th point: what these numbers are measuring is the bandwidth
memory -> CPU -> memory, either just copied or through the FP unit.
They use large enough data sets to avoid significant data cache benefits.]

Well, first of all, I only excerpted some of the more interesting 
machines.  There are still workstations shipping today which get
less than 60 MB/sec.  A large number of desktop workstations and
PC's in the field are in the range 10-60 MB/sec.  Not so long ago,
bandwidth of 60 MB/sec and FP performance in the 3-6 MFLOPS range,
streaming from memory, was considered a very, very hot box.

Second, you have to look at the overall architecture and target applications.
SGI has chosen to go after the high-performance graphics-processor-
oriented graphics market, and, also, has gone after the server
market with some many-processor [up to 36] systems using large caches w/
each processor to reduce memory traffic.  This is a valid approach
to some problems: the last time I checked, SGI servers were in 
the handful of servers right at the top in RDBMS server performance,
for example.  This approach is less useful for memory-intensive
applications, in which I tend to be interested.  And the R-8000
based systems are not that bad- for a time, they were second only
to the IBM numbers.  As I said, these are interesting machines today.

Third, you have to be careful interpreting any benchmark.  For example,
I reboot systems fairly often, and the SGI Indy systems I reboot come
up faster than any desktop I have used [with a "real" OS], despite the 
fact that some numbers would lead to expect otherwise.  I don't know
why they reboot more quickly, and it doesn't seem to matter to many people, 
but it happens to be an important performance criterion *to me*.  YMMV.



