Cliff Click (cliffc@ami.sps.mot.com) wrote:
: sschaem@teleport.com (Stephan Schaem) writes:

: > Well, if the memory is cached: using register only or memory + register
: > is not any diferent, it will be 1 cycle or less (I supose here that
: > the implementation support copyback cache and separate inst/data cache
: > like on the old motorola cisc)
: >   Stephan

: No.  reg+reg uses a lot less resources than reg+mem - in particular it
: avoids the precious load unit which is typically a bottleneck for
: superscalar issue.

	add.l	d4,d0		;16bit instruction
	add.l	(offset,a0),d5	;"	"
	add.l	d1,(offset,a7)	;"	"
	add.l	(offset,pc),d7	;"	" 

 all execute in the same time on a 040, 1 cycle...

: Superscalar issue logic can determine that reg+reg is independent of
: other operations and issue it in parallel.  reg+mem is dependent on
: prior memory operations (store buffers can help avoid a store/load
: stall here), the value must actually *be* in cache (not really an 
: issue since the value got into a register for the reg+reg case), and
: finally: most modern machines have as many, if not more, integer
: units than load/store units.  

 The 060 do have a copyback cache and 3 level FIFO write buffer.
 I do not know tho if it can execute the above 4 instructions in 2 cycles.
 An 040 would take 4 cycles.

: Hence reg+reg typically "disappears" into cycles that are required
: to use the load unit.  Reg+mem puts more pressure on the load unit
: and will prevent parallel issue occasionally.

 I think this can be minimized alot... and usually when the register
 shortage happen the non register variable will be the readonly 
 ones (If possible). 

: This is all moot if you actually only need the "mem" value once;
: in that case even the cache is less helpful.

 Thats true... and usualy if you need that variable once speed is not
 a concern. The diference would be 'bloated' code for the risc version.

 Stephan




