>In article <3172348B.FF6@wight.hursley.ibm.com>,
>Max Waterman  <dwater@hursley.ibm.com> wrote:
>| Raghu Mani wrote:
>
>| > Well, according to DEC's web site, the figures for the 400 MHz alpha are 11.7
>| > and 15.9 (see http://www.digital.com:80/info/semiconductor/dsc-21164.html).
>| > respectively.
>| 
>| NB These are estimates.
>
>Which, given their past track record, are lower than their production
>model performance results.
>
>Roman

The SPEC benchmarks are system level benchmarks, not processor
benchmarks. In means (for SPEC) that they involve at least cache &
memory system as well as the processor. 

Compare these systems with DEC 21164 processors. Note especially
the frequencies:

System			Cache		SPECfp95	Frequency
DEC 500 workstation	2 MB		11.6		333
DEC 500 workstation	2 MB		14.0 (est)	400

DEC 600 workstation	4 MB		13.2		333
DEC 8400 (mainframe)	4 MB		14.2		350

It can be argued that the DEC 600 is somewhat of a "SPEC showpiece"...

For a multiprocessor like the 8400 there is more reason for big caches
to cut down on bus traffic. We are of course talking a different price
level per processor module than for workstations. It also has faster
SRAM in its cache. 

The DEC 500 and DEC 255 workstations (those with the new gray look)
seem nicely designed, particulary for mechanical CAD which seems
to be a use that DEC emphasises for them. That they don't get as high
SPEC per megahertz as the DEC 600 should be less important.

For what it is worth, the 8400 would probably be able to do 15.9
SPECfp at 400 MHz. Though this isn't particulary relevant if you're
looking for a workstation. 

People buy systems, not processors. They run applications, not
benchmarks. 




