>>DEC_8400_5/300            8   899.8     834.2     892.8     978.8
>>DEC_8400_5/300            1   186.3     174.4     198.3     195.2
>
>So much for this being a main memory bandwidth benchmark!
>
Is your problem understanding why more CPUs give better performance ?
These are multiprocessors with pended buses, so more CPUs mean more
simultaneous outstanding bus transactions and hence more memory traffic.
A part of the reason why the IBM 590/990 numbers are so good is that
is a uni-processor with very long cache lines. No SMP system can reasonably
afford the long cache lines they use, especially for commercial workloads.
It should be noted that IBM themselves has been unable for years to
even remotely repeat the memory system of these two machines in anything
newer.

All vendors are striving to get better usable main memory bandwidth
for their machines, it's just not that easy (see Digitals next effort in
that area in a few weeks).

