morphis@fnalv1.fnal.gov wrote:
> RISC is more easily ?pipelined? (I think that is the word) i.e. it is 
> easier for a RISC CPU to have several pipelines executing multiple 
> instructions at the same time.

You're confusing pipelining and superscalarity (unless you mean
`pipeline stages' and not `pipelines'). Pipelining means you chop an
instruction into subtasks, give each to a stage, which allows you to
schedule a new instruction in the first stage as the previous moves to
the next, in an assembly-line fashion. Doesn't decrease the total exec
time of an operation, but does allow you to get more total throughput.

Having more than one pipeline and scheduling more than one instruction
to a pipeline front in any one cycle is only for superscalars. Unl

>CISC can still (usually?) get more done per cycle than RISC

Yes and no. A RISC is still capable of (usually) scheduling 1 or more
instructions per cycle. CISC only wins if the instruction does more
work, and compiled programs typically do not use all of the CISC complex
instructions very often. There are typically mem-to-mem ops, though,
wheras a CISC is often load/store and operations on registers only.

BTW, most recent CISC processors are really RISCs in sheep's clothing
;).

