That 'equipment' consisted of (essentially) a very special disk drive. The
RL02 is an embedded servo drive (one of the first, I think), and uses 'servo
bursts' recorded at the start of each sector to provide position feedback for
the head servo. If those bursts are still intact, it's probably possible to
rewrite the rest of the disk using a somewhat modified RL drive and controller.
If the servo patern is damaged, however, there's no way a normal drive can
position the heads.

The bursts were written on what was essentially a 'stepper positioned'
drive. I suspect (but have no proof) that it used an optical feedback
transducer, a bit like the one in an RK05, and specially-made heads to
record the servo pattern on a blank disk. Then a modified embedded servo
drive put down the rest of the format. I have no proof of this, however.

I am told that modern PC winchesters are factory formatted in the clean room
by linking a 'stepper' to the head positioner, and writing the servo bursts.

This equipment was not at all common - did any customers ever have it? I
got the pickings from a site that did a lot of DEC maintenance, and while
there were extender boards, RK05 alignment packs, skewmaster tapes, KM11's,
etc, there was no formatting equipment.

