In <4klrd3$anr@fnnews.fnal.gov> morphis@fnalv1.fnal.gov wrote:
> 
> CISC can still (usually?) get more done per cycle than RISC
> 
> The lack of registers is far less important for CISC (why? I have no clue.
> anybody care to expound on this?)
> 
RISC machines generally reference memory locations only in load, store, and 
branch instructions.  The other instructions (arithmetic, logic, etc) require 
all operands to be in registers. This allows for 1) simpler instructions (no 
memory referencing needed), 
2) faster access to the operands (helpful for very high clock rated RISC 
cpu's), and 
3) fewer bits required for the instruction (since the register numbers are 
smaller than the memory addresses, and it's a good thing since RISC programs 
tend to have lots more instructions than CISC ones.)

Part of the complex part of CISC machines like the VAX is the ability to have 
the operands for most instructions  to be either memory locations or 
registers.  Of course, the data  from memory still has to be loaded to the 
cpu, but that complication is hidden from the user, and it's done without 
using the normal registers.

Cheers,
Bill Long
UW-Madison Physics
long@phenxb.physics.wisc.edu




