Andrew Remsen wrote:
> 
>  I recently purchased some graphing software to run on my labs AlphaXL 266
> running Windows NT. The software said that it was compatible with WinNT but
> when I loaded it, I got an error message stating that the software wouldn't
> run on a RISC system. Is this something I should continue to look out for or
> will software written for Windows NT run on my alpha? Or should I look for
> software that specifically says AlphaNT compatible.

It seems that "compatible with Windows NT" by default means "compatible with 
Windows NT x86". The software you have is either Win16 code with 386 (or 
greater) instructions in it, or Win32 x86 code. For now, you have unuseable 
software.

However, all is not lost. Windows NT 4.0 RISC (Alpha, MIPS, PowerPC) will have 
386 emulation of Win16 code, so if your software is Win16 code, it should run. 
Furthermore, Windows NT Alpha will have FX!32 that will run x86 Win32 code, so 
if your software is x86 Win32, it should run. MIPS and PowerPC will not have 
FX!32.

Of course, the best option is to get native Alpha Windows NT software, but 
failing that, the two options above should have most bases covered.
