In article <316D713D.5EE9@InternetMCI.com>, Carl Heinlein <Carl.Heinlein@InternetMCI.com> writes:
|We have a chain of store that have MicroVax 2000's. Our corporate 
|headquarter's has a AlphaSever 1000. We want to know how to transfer 
|daily information over the Internet. I don't know very much about the 
|Internet but I have heard you can tranfer data without having to poll 
|each of our stores by making a long-distant call which we currently do.
|
|Carl

Well, the Internet provides global IP-layer data connectivity.
If each site were to get an Internet connect from a local
Internet provider (given your requirements, it sounds like
maybe as little as a $20/month dialup connection for the
remote sites would do - you'd probably want a permanent
56Kb Internet hookup for your main site, at a cost of perhaps
$500/month), you could then indeed transfer data from the 
remote sites to the central one without paying long-distance 
charges.

You'll have some issues to grapple with.  There are the many
technical ones ... how do you get your various (I presume,
VMS) systems to talk over IP?  You could put a TCP/IP stack
on each system ... there are several ones available.  cisco's
(formerly TGV's) MultiNet product gives you this, with SLIP
support that can enable each MicroVAX to handle a modem connection
to the Internet provider.  You'd need to implement your own
scripting (I suggest C-Kermit) to handle the dialup, set up
the network connection, transfer the files and tear down the
connections.

Perhaps more challengingly, you'll have security issues to
deal with.  First, you may not trust the security of your
data to the graces of the various Internet providers and
backbone carriers through whose systems it will flow.  You might
want to encrypt your files before sending them.  Also, you may
want to worry about the accessibility of your systems to
potential intruders out there in the Internet.  A good packet
filtering router (might I suggest a cisco :-) at each
Internet point of presence might be a wise investment.

Finally, there's the reliability issue.  You'll need to check
out your Internet providers and see how reliably you can
make connection attempts to them.  (You may want to get a
permanent connection to each for this reason.)  The reliability
of the Internet backbone providers is pretty good - should
be sturdy enough for nightly batch transfers, though if your
needs involve near-realtime performance, you may want to 
consider other solutions.

The bottom line is that using the Internet may well reduce
your recurring costs in the long run - but you should well
consider the significant up front costs in cash and people
time in getting the hardware, software, network links and
configurations all working.  If you're serious about this,
enlisting the support of a competent consultant would probably
be a good move.

Good luck,

Aaron

