In article <4ko4u7$nq8@nadine.teleport.com>, sschaem@teleport.com (Stephan
Schaem) wrote:

> : CISC can still (usually?) get more done per cycle than RISC
> 
>  Its hard to say per cycle...

Exactly. RISC can usually do more simple instructions/cycle, CISC does
fewer but more complex instructions/cycle. But both have a big element
that is over time a production process.

Mainly RISC is cheaper to manufacture, and quicker to market (simpler to
design in many ways). So it CAN take advantage of higher tech. That does
not mean that it always will.

> : The lack of registers is far less important for CISC (why? I have no clue.
> : anybody care to expound on this?)
> 
>  Cisc are usually not bound to do register to register operation.
>  So you dont have to load all your variable in registers to do operations.
>  And cisc like the 680x0 do have 16 general purpose register, and are
>  alot more eficient at using 'register file' .

The 68K is not the average CISC... we should look at the register starved 80x86.

Yes CISC can do more memory operations... but the processor still must do
the same work in the CISC as the RISC, it still must load the location
into temp area, then process it. The RISC just keeps this as seperate
instructions - which enables the compiler more control over the process.

>  With RISC or CISC the data bandwidth and amount should stay the same.
>  the major change is the size of the L1 cache code size (non unified
>  cache). So for the same overall rating chip (cisc or risc) the risc
>  will require maybe upto 2x time the bandwidth and x2 the size of its
>  code resource.

Huh?!?!

If you look at the PPC, I think your code creap is something like 20% over
the 68K. (Not sure, but that number seems to hold true). So expect that it
takes 20% more code to do the same work. However the data is almost
IDENTICAL (you can align things to be more wasteful and faster, but it is
not required). However because the Processor HAS an L1 and L2 cahces, and
you can afford to add both for just the price of the equivelent CISC CPU,
it seems to me your bandwidth to memory is WAY WAY less (because you have
most hits going to cache).  

Now you can add L1 or L2 to a CISC processor... but then for the same cost
you could add MORE to a RISC processor. So in the real world with the
added cache's of RISC, I beleive the usage of memory is much smaller than
CISC (say 40%). This is mostly speculation (on the exact numbers) but
holds true in theory... if it did not, then RISCS would be slower than
CISC, and this does not seem to be the case.
