morphis@fnalv1.fnal.gov writes:
>	What you wrote above is incorrect, CISC can do far more per 
>instruction than RISC.  

Right.

>RISCs instructions are (typically) simpler and do less

Right. Note however, that most RISCs features 2 x src->destination type
operations, whereas most CISCs uses 2-address type operations, with one
of the addresses acting as both source and destinations. This may make
extra move operations needed on a CISC.

>RISC is more easily ?pipelined? (I think that is the word) i.e. it is easier
>for a RISC CPU to have several pipelines executing multiple instructions
>at the same time.  

Nope:
Pipelining means partitioning instruction execution into multiple
overlapping phases. Using multiple pipelines is a different issue.

>CISC can still (usually?) get more done per cycle than RISC

Nope:
The amount of work done pr cycle depends on the implementation. Both
RISC and CISC chips has been implemented with few (slow) pipestages and
many (fast pipestages), e.g.:

  Architecture		Pipestages		
  Alpha (RISC)		7-9 (-...)		
  Power2 (RISC)		5-...			
  386 (CISC)		3 (i think?)		
  Pentium Pro (CISC)	12-...(or so)

There is ceratinly beeing done more work in a single cycle on a Power2 RISC,
than on Pentium Pro.

>The lack of registers is far less important for CISC (why? I have no clue.
>anybody care to expound on this?)

Actualle, I don't think you are right. The lack of registers is a
problem. It can be reduced by on-the-fly register renaming and out of
order execution - but it is not cheap.

>Well designed RISC will typically get more done per second than CISC...

This is actually simply a statement saying that RISCs are fasters than
CISC. Historically it is true, but the difference is not overwhelming.

>at least for CPU intensive operations.  I suspect I could cobble up a
>situation (say with 16-32 megabytes of memory, and a fair amount of disk io)
>where a CISC processor (say a newer VAX or PPRO) could beat many RISC
>processors.  

I assume this to be because of lower instruction density. But it shouldn't
matter that much.

