morphis@fnalv1.fnal.gov writes:
>hmmm, I had thought that RISC instructions were simple enough that breaking
>them up would be difficult... while I understand that different RISCs
>are (duh!) different, I thought that the ALPHA had one of the simpler
>instruction sets and yet I thought I saw a post earlier in this thread
>stating that the ALPHA had a (relatively) large number of pipeline stages.

A simple pipeline may consist of:
	- Instruction Fetch from cache
	- Instruction Decode and register Read
	- Execute arithmetic operation
	- Acces data cache
	- Write result to register

I several pipelines are operating in parallel (superscalar implementation)
the decoding phase also has to detect and handle dependencies between
simulatneously decoded instructions. This may add extra pipestages. If
the processor supports out-of-ord-execution extra stages may be needed
for various administrative purposes - it is not easy to control a chip with
20 or more instructions in various stages of execution. Finally, some of
the stages above may be split into multiple stages simply to make a higher
clockrate possible. This is sometimes called superpipelining.

