In article <316E741C.1CFB@brussels.sgi.com> Alexis Cousein,
al@brussels.sgi.com writes:
>> RISC is more easily ?pipelined? (I think that is the word) i.e. it is 
>> easier for a RISC CPU to have several pipelines executing multiple 
>> instructions at the same time.
>
>You're confusing pipelining and superscalarity (unless you mean
>`pipeline stages' and not `pipelines'). Pipelining means you chop an
>instruction into subtasks, give each to a stage, which allows you to
>schedule a new instruction in the first stage as the previous moves to
>the next, in an assembly-line fashion. Doesn't decrease the total exec
>

Actually, the first poster's statement was correct.  In order to have
multiple pipelines executing in parallel, the processor must resolve
dependencies
between instructions.  CISC instruction sets tend to have a lot of
problems with
dependencies.  Another problem can be a lack of registers.  Basically,
Intel
pulled every trick in the book to get maximum performance with P6.  They
used
register-renaming which removes false dependencies between instructions
using the
same register.

All processors will have instruction dependencies, but RISC architectures
are
usually designed to avoid this whenever possible, so that fancy
techniques aren't
necessary.

