In article <4kgs5q$su9@nyx10.cs.du.edu>, pciszek@nyx10.cs.du.edu (Paul Ciszek) writes...
>I am using a PDP-11 with two RL02 hard drives.  Some time back, we received
>a gift of two RL02 disks that had never been used.  We initialized one of
>them (INIT/BAD in RT-11) and began storing data on it.  Now we occasionally
>see corrupted files that may or may not be the fault of the disk.

True, it may be the fault of the disk.  But it may also be the disk controller,
flaky memory, or even the fact that your backplane or controller are not
22-bit capable.  Is it on a RLV11 or RLV12 or RL11?  How much memory
is in the system?  Is parity-checking enabled?  Is 22-bit addressing
enabled on the RLV12, if that's a controller you have?  Which monitor
(I'm assuming RT11 here) and version number?

A certain source of file corruption is putting a RLV11 into a Q-bus
system with more than 248 kbytes of memory :-)  Been there, done that!

>  One source
>has suggested that if a disk were formatted then stored for years, the format
>might "fade" in places.

If this was what was happening, I'd expect you to get error messages warning
you of unreadable blocks.  Have any such messages appeared?

> 
>   1) Has anyone ever heard of the need to re-format (as opposed to re-
>      initialize) a RL02 disk?

No.  I have RL01's that are 17 years old now and still have zero bad
blocks, and I have RL02's that are nearly as old.
Physical problems (dust, dirt, media warping) are rather common,
though, especially with poorly stored packs.

Have you run a *thorough* bad block scan on the questionable RL02 packs?
INIT/BAD is a start, but FORMAT/VERIFY:ONLY/PATTERN:777777 is a bit
more thorough.  Even better would be to run the appropriate XXDP+
diagnostics (I think ZRLJ?? or ZRLK?? is the general exerciser.  XXDP+
also will check for addressing problems, which I'd suspect in the case
of file corruption without any error messages.)

>   2) Does anyone still posess the equipment to re-format a RL02?  The
>      DEC representatives I have talked to do not know of anyone who has
>      the equipment.  (Unlike modern hard disks, you need more than just
>      a drive and the operating system to format a hard disk.)

DEC clearly once had such equipment, as well as RL02-compatible manufacturers
like Nashua.

Is it really worthwhile to reformat when RL02's are available
