In article <4ki7qn$m8f@news.csie.nctu.edu.tw>, m8211512@athletes.EE.NCTU.edu.tw (YCYANG) writes:
> Well, since you are from PowerPC division, I assume
> that you have the correct information.

Never, never assume I, personally, know anything about what I happen
to say.  But if you lend me an ear, I can surely take the opportunity
to spin a tale or two.  ;-)

> However from the words in page 506, column 2, line 15,
> I presumed that ALL instructions ICU received will 
> appear on the I-bus.  If not, why they need to be marked
> valid?
>
> (although in page 504, column 2, line 14, it said
> branch instructions never leave the ICU.  I presumed that
> means no-one will use it.)

The FXU & FPU chips only execute the integer & FP instructions.  The
I-bus shown in the figure connecting the ICU chip with the FXU & FPU
chips only communicate integer & FP instructions.  Branch & CR logical
instructions are filtered out and executed on the ICU by the branch &
CR logical units before making it to the I-bus.  Actually, if memory
serves me, I don't think they are actually formally "dispatched", but
rather the branch & CR logical units pluck them directly from the
I-queue on the ICU prior/during dispatch.  Upon dispatch, the FXU &
FPU chips see only a stream of integer & FP instructions.  The valid
bits on the I-bus are used for those rare ;-) occurrences when the
right combination of 4 integer and FP instructions are not available
for dispatch in a given cycle, allowing some subset of 0 to 4
instructions to be dispatched.

