In article <4keum0$clo_001@news.fast.net>, kshuff@fast.net (kshuff) writes:

>   I just acquired a Decstation 3100 and have some questions, since I dont 
>have any documentation. The unit has 12 meg of ram and two hard drives (RZ23 
>and RZ24), what is the capacity of these drives?

RZ23 - ~100 MB
RZ24 - ~200 MB

Between the there is enough space for a pretty minimal installation
of recent ULTRIX version.  Unfortunately, the installation procedure
won't believe that either disk is big enough.  You need about 300 MB
for the installation procedure to believe that it has enough space.

>Also, is it possible to 
>upgrade the unit from mono to color?

Yes.  You need to find the color frame buffer and a suitable (VR290
or similar) monitor.  I don't recall the part number of the color
frame buffer.

>And now for the big question, upon 
>power up I get a failure, to be exact I get 7..6..5..4..3..2.. and hang on 2 
>for a few seconds then responds with failure-reset to continue. After 
>resetting, same thing 7..6..5..4..3..2.. then display's failure, an address of 
>some sort, the drops into console mode. Is this a serious problem? The only 
>things connected are the keyboard, mouse, and monitor.

From my experience three things can cause selftest failures; no mouse,
no Ethernet, unterminated SCSI.  I think test 2 is the Ethernet test.
The DECstation 3100 has two Ethernet connectors; one for thickwire
and one for thinwire.  There's a switch (inset between the two connectors)
to select which you want to use.  Find an appropriate terminator for
one or the other and switch it to use that one.

>I know the BNC 
>connector is for the Eithernet, what are the other ports for?

The connector above the pair of Ethernet connectors is the SCSI
port.  The system has a single SCSI bus.  Internally there are
two or three power and SCSI drops and then the cable runs outside
the box.  If you don't have anything connected outside, use the
terminator there.

As I recollect (I don't have one handy to look at).  There's a
modular connector for the keyboard and one for the mouse.  The
monitor connector should be fairly obvious.  There is either
a pair of 25 pin RS232 connectors or another modular connector
and a single 25 pin RS232.  These are for the remaining serial
ports.  The system has four, one for the mouse, one for the keyboard
and two others.  None support modem control signals and the drivers
only support speeds up to 9600 baud.  I think the interface supports
14.4.
  
