I've got a client who just installed a DEC Alpha which has Digital Unix 3.2 
installed.  I'm trying to setup remote printing from the Alpha to printers 
attached to an IBM RS/6000 running AIX 3.2.  

My expertise is about 90% AIX and 10% HP-UX.  Digital Unix is "new" to me.

I stumbled around and found the 'lprsetup' script.  It asked me a bunch of 
questions that made me think I was on the right track.  The man pages didn't 
work (got a message about not being able to find the 'more' command whenever 
you tried to look up anything with man) so I couldn't look anything up.

We will hopefully be getting the man pages straightened out soon, but in the 
meantime, I was hoping to get a feel for whether I was on the right track.  
Let me re-phrase what I want to do:

RS/6000 has printer attached.  Queue is defined on RS/6000 and local printing 
works fine.  The /etc/host.lpd file includes the name of the Alpha to allow it 
to use the printer on the RS/6000.  And I've got the lpd daemon running on the 
AIX box.  So I think I've got it setup correctly to act as 'print server'.

I want to setup a queue on the Alpha which points to the queue on the RS/6000. 
 On AIX this simply amounts to (though I usually set it up via the sysadmin 
tool, smit) a couple of stanzas in the /etc/qconfig file which point to the 
print server by its hostname and the name of the queue on the print server.

Questions:
--------------
??? Is 'lprsetup' the right command to setup a queue on the Alpha to direct 
printing to the RS/6000 print server?

??? Do you know what the file comparable to /etc/qconfig on the Alpha is?

??? This one is a little off the wall: If I have standalone print server box 
(such as that made by Gold Box; I hear HP's doesn't support Digital Unix for 
some strange reason) which advertises itself thusly:

        TCP/IP users see the Gold Box as a host with a unique IP
        address running the lpd line printer daemon; any host computer
        which supports the Berkeley remote-LPR command can spool
        jobs to the Gold box.  It also supports bootp and rarp and can be
        configured and managed via TELNET.

..can I setup a queue on the Alpha in a similar fashion as for setting it up 
to talk to a printer on the RS/6000?



