Steve Bakke wrote:
> 
> In article <4js9jr$71p@agate.berkeley.edu> Peter Mardahl,
> peterm@landau.EECS.Berkeley.EDU writes:
> >I don't know where you got the idea that pipeline stages make the
> >CPU more difficult to upclock.  If anything, the opposite is true.
> >
> >The 21164A has 7 integer pipeline stages and 9 fp pipeline stages.
> >
> >The 21164A is also the highest-clocked CPU in the world right now
> >at 400MHz.
> >
> >Regards,
> >
> >PeterM
> 
> You are confusing pipeline stages with separate pipelines.  More stages
> generally lowers clock speed.  More pipes increases complexity, though
> won't necessarily increase clock speed.

More stages means shorter/smaller/simpler steps. Therfore each
step can be completed in a shorter period of time. So, since
each step in the pipeline takes a shorter period of time, the
clock can run faster. Of course it will then take more clocks
for an instruction to get from the first stage to the last, but
the idea behind the whole thing is that you can insert a new
instruction into the pipeline and have one completed by the
pipeline each clock cycle. And everything that is happening
in the middle piplines are happening in paralell and are 
different.

You can then put multiple pipelines in paralell and get 2 or 3
instructions to complete per clock cycle. Then you have to make
a special compiler to optimize the code to remove data dependancies
and stuff so that you never have to check for that stuff in
the processor. Sometimes that compiler has to insert alot of
NOPs that slow down the code, but no more than what would have
to be done if the processor had to flush the pipeline. Also,
making the compiler deal with those problems makes it so the
CPU doesn't thus decreasing the complexity of the CPU.

