In article <1996Apr2.151749.4173@cronkite.res.utc.com>, 
sondak@galileo.res.utc.com (Doug Sondak	) writes:
|>Although I am using an IBM workstation, I work closely with a
|>colleague who has a cluster of DEC Alpha workstations.  We
|>generate unformatted files in our fortran code, but we can't
|>share these files due to the different binary formats on the
|>machines.  (I routinely transfer files between IBM and SGI
|>workstations, but I assume that the DEC words have the bits
|>swapped compared to these machines.)

I think I responded to Doug's question in another forum/newsgroup - in
brief, the solution is to use -convert big_endian on the Alpha side when
compiling the Fortran code. This will make unformatted files automatically
convert to the IBM-style big-endian byte order.  There are "smaller hammers"
available to specify this for individual units - see the DEC Fortran
User Manual for details.
