In article <4jg4o2$vuk@news.interserve.com.hk>, (someone with no name) writes:

|>VAX7800 is the newest VAX7000 model supporting VMS 5.5-2. But it is funny 
|>that I could not find any software or hardware certified on this 
|>platform.
|>
|>Could any one please tell me if VAX7800 supports any common Digital 
|>layered product, such as ALLIN1, PSI, PSI Access, Message Router, MR 
|>Gateway, DECps, etc. Besides, if anyone knows what DECintact is, I would 
|>like to know if DECintact can run on VMS 5.5-2 under VAX7800.

The problem is that the "certification" (I assume you are referring to the
Software Support Addendum that lists the processors supported) is valid
at the time it is written, but doesn't get updated for new processors
released afterwards until the next layered product revision. 

In general, you will find that any layered product that doesn't have 
peculiar hardware dependencies is supported on "Any VAX system supported
by OpenVMS".  Some layered products (Fortran, for example) are switching
to wording of this form, as it is just too darned difficult keeping up with
all the new processor models, all of which, from our perspective, are alike.
"A VAX is a VAX is a VAX", and that sentiment (with appropriate substitution)
pretty much goes for Alpha systems supported by OpenVMS too.

As for DECintact, the SPD starts out as follows:

 The DECintact product provides a foundation for building simple and
 complex transaction processing applications on one or more VAX sys-
 tems under the OpenVMS VAX operating system. The DECintact product runs
 as a collection of services and several processes under the OpenVMS
 VAX operating system. DECintact supports either single-threaded (per-
 process) or multithreaded (server) application program design method-
 ologies. Multiple versions of the DECintact product can execute at the
 same time and share the same physical memory while remaining completely
 independent. 

For more info, see ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/SPD/29-58-05.txt

DECintact is supported on OpenVMS V5.5-2, so it should have no problems on
a 7000-800, which is just a faster 7000-700.  We have testing groups whose
task is to test layered products on any new processor.  We take
cross-model compatibility VERY seriously.
