James Shen wrote:
> 
> In article <1996Mar27.195932@carl.a-com.com>,
>   byer@carl.a-com.com (Robert Byer) writes:
> > I have a problem, I have a VAX 6340, a VAXstation-3100 and three
> > MicroVAX-II systems all clustered together running VMS 5.5-2HW.
> > They all see each other, they all serve their disks and tapes to
> > each other and all is well.  With the following problem.
> >
> > When I configured all the machines I told it that their was not
> > quorum disk.  Now, if I have to shut one of the machines down and
> > I run the SHUTDOWN.CON file and tell it to REMOVE_NODE it shuts
> > the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
> > cluster hang and I have to reboot them.  The system console displays
> > that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
> > reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
> > stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

	It is my understanding (been wrong before) that quorum can never be 
adjusted down.  If you don't have a quorum disk and lose a node then the 
cluster will hang until the original quorum is reestablished.

	We have a quorum disk in our cluster.  Each node is given one vote 
and the quorum disk is configured to have (number of nodes/2) + 1 vote 
rounded up i.e. - (4 nodes / 2) + 1 = 3 votes.  This means we can loose all 
nodes but one and still keep the cluster running.  We can also lose the 
quorum disk and keep running.  Since ALL our disks are off HSC style 
controllers the disk is available as long as it's good.

	My .02 worth.

jcs



