Subject: Re: HELP!!! VAX Cluster Quorum Disk Problem...
Date: 1996/04/05
Message-ID: <4k3m7h$pvm@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 145974599
distribution: world
references: <1996Mar27.195932@carl.a-com.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.sysmgt


In article <1996Mar27.195932@carl.a-com.com>, byer@carl.a-com.com (Robert Byer) writes:

:I have a problem, I have a VAX 6340, a VAXstation-3100 and three
:MicroVAX-II systems all clustered together running VMS 5.5-2HW.

  OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2HW predates the V5.5-2 release.   (It's old.)
  The current OpenVMS VAX release is V7.0.

:They all see each other, they all serve their disks and tapes to
:each other and all is well.  With the following problem.
:
:When I configured all the machines I told it that their was not
:quorum disk. 

  As there appear to be dual-path disks, there is no need nor reason
  for using a quorum disk -- since each disk is accessable via exactly
  one node, the votes that might have been "given" to the quorum disk
  can be (better) given to the serving node.

: Now, if I have to shut one of the machines down and
:I run the SHUTDOWN.CON file and tell it to REMOVE_NODE it shuts
:the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
:cluster hang and I have to reboot them. 

  Unless the node is going away more or less permanently, the SHUTDOWN
  REMOVE_NODE option is not particularly necessary.

  If _the_ voting node -- I will assume that _the_ voting node is the
  VAX 6000-340 -- goes away, then the cluster hang is normal and is
  expected behaviour.

:The system console displays
:that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
:reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
:stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

  You will want to make _absolutely_ certain that the setting of
  EXPECTED_VOTES is equal to the total number of votes from all
  nodes (and, if one is configured) the quroum disk.  Satellite
  nodes typically do not get configured with votes -- votes are
  normally configured on the nodes that are expected to be up and
  operating, and that have direct (local) access to the system disk.

:What do I have set wrong and how to I remove a node from the cluster
:so I don't take the whole cluster down.

  Most obviously, don't use REMOVE_NODE unless it is applicable here:
  see the system management documentation for information here.  On
  non-voting node SHUTDOWNs, REMOVE_NODE is not typically used...

  You'll want to provide information on which nodes have a local
  (non-downline-loaded) system disk, and which node(s) have vote(s),
  and how many.  Information on the quorum disk and -- if one or more
  CI or DSSI connections is involved -- node and storage controller
  disk and tape allocation class settings.

  You'll also want to take a look at the VMScluster documentation.

  ------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------




