In article <1996Mar31.222806.13461@nb.rockwell.com> Michael M.Y. Hui,
huimm@nb.rockwell.com writes:
>But Java is CISC. Are you suggesting we
>reverse technological progress and return to early
>1980's with the beautifully designed iAPX-432?
>-- 
>Michael M.Y. Hui (speaking privately) myhui@thlayli.newport-beach.ca.us

What do you mean Java is CISC?  Having a chip be Java-optimised has
nothing
to do with that.  All the chip needs to do is to support a few certain
operations more than the average general-purpose CPU.

I don't want to go into a discussion of CISC vs. RISC.  Most people do not
really understand what the differences are.  Nowadays it is mostly an
over-used marketing term.

Here's my definition of a good "RISC" machine:

1. Instructions are all of equal length.
2. All memory operations are simple loads or stores.
3. There is an abundance of registers on-chip.
4. There are a limited number of addressing modes.
5. The processor will not have a single status register.

All of the above make pipelining and superscalar execution much
simpler.  Now, all that is needed is a few instructions which would
speed up the interpretation of Java scripts.

