dwater@wight.hursley.ibm.com writes:
>Z80. I think there is a discussion in comp.benchmarks about this, and it
>was suggested that the Alpha, although one of (if not the) top performer
>in terms of specmarks, its specmark/mhz ratio was substantially lower
>than some others. I can't remember whic was top - something mildy
>obscure - ie not MIPS/Intel/Alpha - don't think it was PowerPC but it
>might have been.
>
>Can anyone remind me?
>
>It may be valid to compare the same processor using clock frequencies ie
>"R4400/150 is slower than R4400/200" would be valid.
>

SPECmark/MHz rating is really irrelevant.  All that matters is the end
performance.  The Alpha
architecture was designed for high-speed implementations.  Therefore,
each instruction does
less, but is also simpler.  This makes it easier to create deep pipelines
and high clock speeds.
The 21164 floating point pipeline is something like 9 stages deep.  The
integer is seven.  That
is longer than pretty much anything.

