We've stumbled on a phenomenon that is cramping our throughput, and we
someone else has seen and solved the problem.



Environment: SAP R3 3.0b under Oracle, running in a 3-tier configurati
DEC Alpha machines networked on an Ethernet LAN, using multiple dialog
processors (D's) to handle an electronic data interchange (EDI) worklo
The number of enabled D's varied from 6 to 30, with similar results ex
for total run time.  During these tests, the only online work was in t
form of sporadic monitoring activities. Because the EDI transmissions 
presented as a flat file, the work approximates a pool of very rapid d
entry clerks: the work queues are always full until the input file is 
completed.



We used the X-windows xload facility to produce parallel displays of '
busy' plotted against time for both the application server and the dat
server.  We expected both displays to show relatively constant levels 
CPU activity.  Instead, each display was a series of sawtooth peaks an
valleys, ranging from a minimum near 25% to a maximum that varied betw
70% and 100%, depending on the machine and the number of dialog proces
enabled. Furthermore, the activity "flowed" between the two systems: w
the app server was at a peak, the db server was at a valley, and vice 
versa.



Every valley represents system cycles that could improve our throughpu
we could smooth out the system utilization.  Any suggestions?  TIA.

-- 
- Rich



