For reasons best known to Digital Semiconductor, the firm has dropped the
"A" suffix from its processor shrinks. The 366 MHz, 375 MHz, 400 MHz, and
500-533 MHz parts are in fact what would have been called 21164A since
they
are CMOS6 optical shrinks of the original EV5 architecture (with some
additional byte word ordering instructions thrown in).

If you look at Alpha history, DEC announced EV4 in Feb 92 and began
shipping at the end of the year. EV5 was announced in Sept 94 and began
shipping in mid-95. If past is prologue, an EV6 announcement this Fall
(perhaps in conjunction with the Microprocessor Forum show) would be a bit
early, but not out of the question. 


>         As for the numbers, Terry Shannon had posted a "Shannon knows DEC" 
>         sampler in various groups around March 6th.  He mentions 1000
>         Specint.  I cannot recall where I read 30 million transistors.
>         Perhaps someone else can remember a source.

DEC claims that EV6 will deliver 1000 SPECint92 and 1500 SPECfp92. This
does not necessarily mean that the first implementation will be this fast.
Several years ago, DEC showed an Alpha roadmap which credited EV6 with 30M
transistors. They haven't said much about transistor count lately, so time
will tell. (But who cares how many transistors the thing has so long as it
works!)

What **will** be interesting is the level of superscalarity that EV6
demonstrates. EV4 was dual-issue, EV5 is quad-issue, could EV6 be 8-way?
Or just six-way? Enquiring compiler designers want to know!

