I think it was the person who posted before me who was confused.

I also think you are confused.  Pipelining a datapath makes it much
easier to upclock:  as a simple example consider this:

You have an operation, an ADD.  This operation takes 10ns, say, with
the fastest implementation/algorithm you can think of.

If you only work on one add per clock, your maximum clock frequency
would be 100MHz.

Now suppose you break your 10 ns add into 5 stages, each of 2 ns,
and PIPELINE the add operations.  You are now working on FIVE
adds per clock tick.  It still takes 10ns to do an add, but
you can have up to FIVE in varying stages of execution.

How fast can your clock be now?  500MHz.  Can you see that
an add is completed by the CPU every 2ns?

In practice it's hard to keep pipelines full however.

Having separate pipelines wasn't being discussed, though
perhaps that is what the guy meant.  I won't comment on
whether superscalarity makes it really that much harder
to upclock a CPU, however, I'll make this remark:
the Alpha 21164A can do up to 4 instructions per clock,
and they have it running at 400MHz, the highest clock
speed of any CPU around.

Regards,
PeterM
