>  > ISBN 1-55558-130-7) comments on this subject in Chapter 9: Comparing
>  > RISC Implementations:
> 
> Actually it does have some application to the area of the memory
> bandwidth limited system which is increasingly becoming a problem.  As
> you ramp the clock speed you still have to get the processor fed.  The
> problem is that all of the extremely high MHZ CPU depend on very good
> cache locality.  If they don't get it they die, literally.  The alpha
> has an incredible appetite and I'd be willing to bet that it spend
> most of its time waiting to be fed.

Be careful here, you may be confusing clock frequency with instruction 
fetch rate.  Although the Alpha runs faster than PowerPC, it doesn't 
retire as many instructions per clock, therefore needs less instruction 
memory bandwidth per clock than PowerPC.  Perhaps a better way to look 
at this is "code-size/time".  (This may spark the resurgence of compact 
instruction sets.)

If the Alpha spends most of its time "waiting to be fed" while 
benchmarking so well against the competition then DEC will certainly be 
hard to catch when synchronous/RAMBus memory systems become more popular 
in the near future.  The slower clock multi-issue folks will be tearing 
their hair out trying to retire 20 instructions/clock to keep up.  Or 
maybe Exponential will shock us all by hitting 500MHz with their spin of 
the PPC604.

Any way you look at it, tomorrow's going to be fun!

Cheers,
Scott



