In article <4jtrmv$1mki@news.gate.net>, solution@gate.net (Ken Walter) wrote:

> Another artical I read some months back (don't remember where) also had an 
> analysis of risc vs. cisc and found after adjusting for architecture which 
> could be implimented on both and implementation technology that Riscs were
> at best only marginally faster than Ciscs, mostly do the the extra cache 
> misses.

RISC is overhyped in some ways. It is a design philosophy more than
anything else. Basically it says that we will simplify the design so that
we can leverage newer technologies faster - and given that technology is
changing so quickly, we can stay more on the crest of the curve. Also it
is a way to REDUCE the CPU costs, so that you have more money to spend on
things like L2 costs (or even L1). 

Those things ARE working. A RISC chip costs less to create and you get
better results/dollar of design and /dollar period - and on
performance/watt. But that is not to say that those are compelling enough
by themselves. They are only an adjusting factor. 

If you can spend 10 times the R&D as a RISC manufacturer - you can still
make a CISC that is as fast or faster. There also seems to be a threshhold
where if both types spend over a certain amount then the CISC can keep up
enough with the RISC as to blunt the advantages. But none of that changes
that RISC is still likely a better design philosophy - wether it holds up
in the real world or not. (Capitalism is better than communism - but
implementations of communism have taken generations to fall).

Let us see when the next technology change happens who adapts faster. Lets
see if BiPolar (exponential) can do what they claim?!?! Or if someone
creates some wafer-scale integration inexpensively who does better
overall. Its a new marathon and CISC advocates think that because Intel
has not lost in the first mile that they've won.
