In article <4jtrmv$1mki@news.gate.net>, solution@gate.net (Ken Walter) wrote:

> ...Riscs were
> at best only marginally faster than Ciscs, mostly do the the extra cache 
> misses.

While this is true for most code, the most time-consuming code can be
optimized to do more work per memory access.  It is somewhat like being
able to write custom microcode for a CISC processor, and not much easier,
but it is available.

For example, my Mandelbrot program runs much faster on RISC, because it is
compute-bound and not memory-bound.  If I coded it naively, to do only one
iteration per cell each time, it would be rather slow.  Instead, I do as
many as I can up to the next display contrast change, and loop like mad.

So, unoptimized code may look about the same speed, but optimized for slow
memory, it can run much faster.
