Scott Kroeger (scott.kroeger@mei.com) wrote:
: If the Alpha spends most of its time "waiting to be fed" while 
: benchmarking so well against the competition then DEC will certainly be 
Alpha only does good benchmark with high memory bandwidth system. It is fast
only if it has a big on board cache such that most of the memory access can
fall into on-chip or on-board cache. If your data set is larger than second
level cache then alpha will become very slow.  I have used STREAM benchmark
to test the performance of memory subsystem of some alpha box and found that
alpha REALLY spends MOST of its time wating for data from main memory if
it is not cached. If the data is not cached, a ldt instruction need about 50
cycles to load a 64bit floating point data from main memory in a 166MHz UDB, it
need about 27 cycles in a 2100 4/275.
And many of FP intensive scientific applications have very large data set.
If you don't a big cache you application will run very slow. Even the cache
size is larger than your data set, conflict cache miss can also slow down
your application and makes the performance of your computer become very
unstable. To take advantage of your high speed alpha chip, you have to 
invest a lot of money on the memory subsystem. My 166MHz UDB has a small cache
(256KB), when I run a program with about 50KB data, it runs like a 166MHz
Pentinum, if the program has about 500KB data, it is slow down and runs like
a 66MHz Pentinum.
If high bandwidth low latency memory systems become more popular and its
price can be cut down, alpha will have a great future.
