Ron Fox <fox@rudolf.nscl.msu.edu> wrote:
>   Subject line says most of it... I'm trying to add a quantum Grand Prix
> 4.3 Gbyte disk to an AXP/150 Alpha PC running Digital Unix (formerly OSF/1).
> The hardware recognizes the existence of the drive (SHOW DEVICE at the >>>).
> Unix recognizes the existence of the drive, and disklabel does let me
> partition the drive and write the label block.  However if I do a newfs
> to create a file system on the drive, then the system hangs and indicates
> SCSI bus timeouts and RESETS.   The drive's select light is continuously lit
> during all of this.
>   Is there some special configuration thing I need to do to get this
> drive to work?

The same result has happened to me with a Quantum Atlas (4Gb) drive.
We have an adaptec AHA-1742A SCSI controller in the DEC model 2000/300,
and the OS is Digital Unix 3.2C . We have found out, that there is
some problem with the burst read, since intensive reading processes
like fsck hang up the SCSI bus. You can write to the disk at a very good
transfer rate, but slow read access (like ls -R) is successful only.
We put the disk to our Novell server, and it works fine. In addition
it has the same SCSI controller.

Tom Varga

