From: rkoehler@csc.com (Robert Koehler)
Subject: Re: HELP!!! VAX Cluster Quorum Disk Problem...
Date: 1996/04/01
Message-ID: <4joqe6$nd8@explorer.csc.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 145236936
references: <1996Mar27.195932@carl.a-com.com>
followup-to: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.sysmgt
organization: Computer Sciences Corporation
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.sysmgt

Robert Byer (byer@carl.a-com.com) wrote:
: -----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


: Help!!!

: I have a problem, I have a VAX 6340, a VAXstation-3100 and three
: MicroVAX-II systems all clustered together running VMS 5.5-2HW.
: They all see each other, they all serve their disks and tapes to
: each other and all is well.  With the following problem.

: When I configured all the machines I told it that their was not
: quorum disk.  Now, if I have to shut one of the machines down and
: I run the SHUTDOWN.CON file and tell it to REMOVE_NODE it shuts
: the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
: cluster hang and I have to reboot them.  The system console displays
: that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
: reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
: stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

: What do I have set wrong and how to I remove a node from the cluster
: so I don't take the whole cluster down.

If one node is taking the quorum, it must have most of the votes, or the number
of expected votes is too high.  Redistribute the votes in a more even fashion, 
and recalculate the number of expected votes (a SYSGEN parameter).

Quorum is the first odd value equal to or greater than half the expected votes 
(integer calcuation (expected_votes + 1)/2).  If all your nodes have a vote of
1, set your expected votes to 5, your quorum would be 3, you could take down
any two nodes without losing quorum.

Actually, expected votes is effectively raised if there are more votes than
the SYSGEN parameter is set to, so you could set expected votes to 3, which
would allow quorum to reach when 3 nodes come up if all were down, but when
the other two come up the value will go up to 5.

When quorum is lost, you can tell the cluster to continue.  At the console:

  break (has to be enabled)
  >>> d/i 12 c
  >>> c
  IPC> Q
  IPC> ^z

This will force the quorum to be recalculated based on the currently available
votes instead of SYSGEN's expected votes.  When the quorum is not lost, 
expected votes can be forced to recalculate via set cluster/expected_votes, but
I don't think that will help in your case.

In your case a quorum disk will not help.

