> I run the SHUTDOWN.CON file and tell it to REMOVE_NODE it shuts
> the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
> cluster hang and I have to reboot them.  The system console displays
> that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
> reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
> stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

I used to say I was an expert at this, but not any more because I've 
been out of it for awhile.

If you have 5 machines, they are all voting members, and each machine 
has 1 vote, then taking any single machine down shouldn't be a problem.  
Check SYSGEN parameters VOTES and EXPECTED_VOTES on each system.  At our 
site we ignore the proper setting for EXPECTED_VOTES and leave it at 2 
in a 7-node cluster because we like to stagger the system startups when 
everything is down and if EXPECTED_VOTES was set to 7 we'd have to boot 
4 systems before they'd actually boot.

