In article <4jpa4k$mce@agate.berkeley.edu>, peterm@landau.EECS.Berkeley.EDU 
(Peter Mardahl) writes:
|>Jon Katz wrote:
|>> its specmark/mhz ratio was substantially lower
|>>than some others.
|>
|>One interesting note is that these 'more efficent' processors can NOT
|>necessarily be upclocked to run at the same speed as an Alpha.  
|>
|>Somewhere along the line, a decision was made to go to a lower clock-speed,
|>more-work-per-clock design instead of something like the Alpha.

Correct - "SPECmark/MHz" is a meaningless measure, sort of like rating
track sprinters on the basis of their stride.  Different processors take
different approaches to getting the job done.  Dr. Dileep Bhandarkar, in his
book "Alpha Implementations and Architecture" (Digital Press,
ISBN 1-55558-130-7) comments on this subject in Chapter 9: Comparing
RISC Implementations:

	"John Mashey of MIPS Computer Inc. used SPECmark/MHz as an
	efficiency metric to compare first-generation RISC systems
	in 1989.  Since early RISC machines had similar pipeline
	organizations, the metric did expose differences in compiler
	sophistication or other deficiencies.  With the diverse
	implementation styles extant today, SPECint92/MHz (or
	SPECfp92/MHz) is no longer a valid measure of efficiency,
	but it is useful as an indicator of different design
	philosophies.

	...

	"Well designed RISC processors must achieve significantly
	higher clock rates or lower CPI [Cycles Per Instruction].
	The designs that emphasize clock speed (Alpha, R4000) fall
	into a quadrant (high clock rate, high CPI) labeled by
	Microprocessor Report as Speed Demons, while wide issue
	designs such as Power and SuperSPARC land in a quadrant
	(low CPI, low clock rate) labeled as Braniacs.  More
	conventional RISC designs such as the PA-RISC processors
	fall somewhere in the middle.  The Speed Demons have
	consistently reached a new performance plateau before their
	Braniac counterparts."
-- 

