In article <1996Mar27.195932@carl.a-com.com>, byer@carl.a-com.com (Robert Byer) writes:
> When I configured all the machines I told it that their was not
> quorum disk.  Now, if I have to shut one of the machines down and
> I run the SHUTDOWN.CON file and tell it to REMOVE_NODE it shuts
> the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
> cluster hang and I have to reboot them.  The system console displays
> that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
> reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
> stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

What are the following on each node in your cluster:

Votes, expected votes, quorum?

Try the following sequence of commands:

$ show cluster /contin
add cluster
add votes
add expected
add quorum

Look for inconsistencies in expected votes and quorum.

Which node(s) are you trying to shut down? have you tried the shutdown
without the remove_node option? I don't think I've ever had luck with that
option myself.

For the cluster you've described, I'd probably give any non-satellite node
one vote, and your large system votes = (voting nodes) -1. In this
configuration, you can run with the big system and any one (or more) node,
OR ALL of the little nodes if the big system is down. Losing multiple small
nodes would not hang your cluster unless they ALL went away.

