> the selected machine down, and then the rest of the machines in the
> cluster hang and I have to reboot them.  The system console displays
> that the selected node is being removed, and the cluster is being
> reconfigured.  Then it says something about the quorum disk and then
> stops with a message like "quorm lost, blocking activity".

       First, Not enough information for _anyone_ who is willing to help
  you.  You need to tell us how they clustered, either mixed interconnect or 
  just NI ? What is the cluster expected votes ? How each machine contributes 
  the vote towards the cluster ? 

       Second, what is the _EXACT_ OPCOM message about the quorum disk ?
  One can find out the cluster quorum information simply by:
  	$ SHO CLUSTER/CONTI
  	Command> ADD CLUSTER, QD_NAME
  This will show you the cluster votes information, including cluster
  expected votes, cluster quorum, cluster votes, quorum disk name if one has
  one and quorum disk votes contributed.  Then tell us what do you see.

       Third, you do _NOT HAVE TO_ reboot the rest of the systems just
  because of quorum lost,  you can boot the system back to the cluster to
  restore the quorum, but I guess this might not be your disired solution,
  since you intend to move the system out of the cluster for a considerable
  period of time.  Another option will be reboot a node with addtional
  votes instead of rebooting every machine.

       Forth, are you sure there is no quorum disk in the cluster ?  If
  you are not sure, is there any chance the system shut down is a quorum
  disk watcher ?  Also, it is _important_ to tell us what is the sysgen
  params on each of the boxes, such as VAXCLUSTER, RECNXINTERVAL,
  QDSKINTERVAL, VOTES, EXPECTED_VOTES ...

       Finally, I _strongly_ recommend you to refer to the BIBLE `VMScluster
  Systems for OpenVMS'.  No offense, but it seems to me that you have not
  gone through the _MANUAL_ yet. 
   
