 > different approaches to getting the job done.  Dr. Dileep Bhandarkar, in his
 > book "Alpha Implementations and Architecture" (Digital Press,
 > ISBN 1-55558-130-7) comments on this subject in Chapter 9: Comparing
 > RISC Implementations:

Actually it does have some application to the area of the memory
bandwidth limited system which is increasingly becoming a problem.  As
you ramp the clock speed you still have to get the processor fed.  The
problem is that all of the extremely high MHZ CPU depend on very good
cache locality.  If they don't get it they die, literally.  The alpha
has an incredible appetite and I'd be willing to bet that it spend
most of its time waiting to be fed.

 > 	fall somewhere in the middle.  The Speed Demons have
 > 	consistently reached a new performance plateau before their
 > 	Braniac counterparts."
This is also incorrect.  It is only in the recent 6 months that people
have started selling systems that can beat the Power2 machines in FP
performance.  For an extemely long time the Power2 machines ( a
Braniac) held the lead on the FP game.  The alpha has yet to ride on
top of this wave as long as the POWER2 did and it looks like it will
fall to either the 200MHZ Ultra or PA-8K at the end of the year.  One
interesting thing to think about is that the Speeddemons tend to have
less memory bandwidth.  Also while the Speeddemons are pushing the
limit of their memory subsystems with their cycle times the Braniacs
still have a long way to go.  It will be interesting to see what is on
top in the year 1998.  Also the main advantage the Alpha has is not in
Architecture but in implementation.  Being a fully dynamic CPU is what
allows it to run at such high speeds.  As other people become more
adventurous and create fully dynamic CPU's it will be interesting to
see if alpha still has such an advantage.  



